WEBVTT

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:07.310
 This is Delicious Revolution, a show about food.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:13.260
 I believe gardens are a space where people can reimagine what the city is and should be.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:22.360
 So the built environment, the city is not just a container, it's not just the playground on which we play out our lives.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:30.000
 We have an active role in shaping what the city looks like and what our relationship is to that land.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.220
 And through creating gardens, maintaining gardens, even fighting for the space to have a garden or to continue that garden,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:45.480
 we're contributing to what Lefebvre and many theorists today call the right to the city,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:58.640
 or what I'd consider the right to imagine and enact the kind of relationships to land and space we want to see in our homes, our broader homes.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:08.350
 Delicious Revolution is a show about food, culture, and place, made by Chelsea Wills and me, Devon Samson.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:14.230
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of the food movement, including farmers,

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.850
 chefs, artists, and activists, and who have a vision for a different food system.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:24.710
 This first season of Delicious Revolution, we talk to friends who are deeply engaged with many aspects of food,

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.650
 and who have inspired us over years with their thoughts and stories.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:45.020
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:49.340
 This is Chelsea Wills, here today with Michelle Gloa at her home in Santa Cruz, California.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:56.710
 Michelle Gloa is an assistant professor in anthropology and social change at the California Institute of Integral Studies.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:05.650
 Her research interests include critical political ecology, urban social movements, and agri-food studies.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:15.620
 Her work uses interdisciplinary frameworks to explore dynamics between activists engaged in changing the landscapes of cities and food systems,

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.730
 and the contemporary institutions with which they interact.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:30.300
 Michelle approaches her research with over a decade of experience working with food justice and urban agriculture organizing in the United States and Mexico.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:40.480
 Specifically, she focuses on the dynamics of land access and property rights, shifting land use and development in food justice organizing.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:48.310
 Michelle received her B.S. in natural resource management and political science from Colorado State University,

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:52.930
 and her Ph.D. in environmental studies from the University of California, Santa Cruz.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:59.360
 Michelle and I met while working together in a research cluster focusing on critical sustainabilities,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:05.250
 looking at the real and imagined past, present, and future of what sustainability is in California.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.890
 I want to begin today by recognizing and remembering activist and urban garden champion,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.420
 Grace Lee Boggs, who just died at 100 years old in her house in Detroit.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.210
 When I started thinking about our chat today, I kept thinking about her,

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:22.490
 and the way her words feed into the conversation we are about to have.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:27.170
 She said, "The time has come for us to reimagine everything.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:31.030
 We have to reimagine work and go away from labor.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.500
 We have to reimagine revolution and get beyond protest.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.260
 We have to think not only about change in our institutions, but changes in ourselves."

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.330
 So welcome to the show, Michelle.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:42.010
 Thank you.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:46.180
 So let's start by talking about urban agriculture.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:51.260
