 a different way to understand my relationship to time and space.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:15.080
 And that personal connection keeps me coming back. But I also have another connection where I believe

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:21.720
 gardens are a space where people can reimagine what the city is and should be and what their connection to

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:31.230
 the broader urban landscape may be. So the built environment, the city is not just a container,

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:39.460
 it's not just the playground on which we play out our lives. We have an active role in shaping

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:46.770
 what the city looks like and what our relationship is to that land. And through creating gardens,

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:52.510
 maintaining gardens, even fighting for the space to have a garden or to continue that garden,

00:07:52.510 --> 00:08:01.310
 we're contributing to what Lefebvre and many theorists today call the right to the city or what I

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:10.330
 consider is people contributing it to the right to the city, to the right to imagine and enact the kind

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:16.770
 of relationships to land and space we want to see in our homes, our broader homes.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:20.200
 And so how is that different from something like a city park?

